### Pick 7 — Lab 2ct in the Same Setting: $4,990

**Setting:** [Pavé Edge in 14K White Gold — $2,180](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/mens-diamond-pave-edge-wedding-ring-in-14k-white-gold-1-6-ct-tw-item-196242?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Stone:** [IGI D-VVS1 2ct Lab-Grown Round — $2,810](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28629934?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Total: $4,990**

Same setting as Pick 6. Lab-grown 2ct stone instead of natural 1ct. The face-up size goes from 6.5mm to 8.1mm. The total goes from $5,420 to $4,990 — $430 less for 25% more stone. The natural equivalent (a 2ct GIA stone) starts at $17,490. See [Men's Lab-Grown Diamond Ring](/mens-lab-grown-diamond-ring) for how to verify IGI certificates before purchase.

The stone is IGI D-VVS1 2ct Ideal cut. D color, VVS1 clarity, lab-certified. No one will tell it apart from a natural stone without a lab report and a loupe. The face-up fire and brightness are identical. The resale value is near zero — lab stones do not hold commodity value.

This is a decision about intent. If the ring is a visual statement and not an asset, Pick 7 gives you more stone for less money than any natural option in this guide.

**What you give up:** No GIA cert. IGI is accurate for lab-grown but is not GIA. The stone has no resale commodity value. If you sell it, expect 10–20% of purchase price.

**What you get:** 8.1mm face-up for $4,990. The same size as a [men's 2 carat diamond ring](/mens-2-carat-diamond-ring) that costs $17,490 in natural GIA inventory.

**Verdict:** Buy this ring if the visual is the goal and the asset is not. It is the most stone per dollar in this guide, by a wide margin.

