## Natural vs Lab — The $5K Decision

At $5,000, the natural vs lab question is the most important decision you will make. Here is what the numbers say.

| Path | Stone | Cert | Size | Total | Resale |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Natural (Pick 1) | G-VS1 1ct | GIA | 6.5mm | $4,530 | 40–50% retained |
| Natural (Pick 5) | D-VS2 1ct | GIA | 6.5mm | $5,316 | 40–50% retained |
| Lab (Pick 7) | D-VVS1 2ct | IGI | 8.1mm | $4,990 | 10–20% retained |
| Lab (Pick 8) | D-FL 1.5ct | IGI | 7.4mm | $4,709 | 10–20% retained |
| Lab (Pick 9) | D-FL 1.5ct | IGI | 7.4mm | $4,800 | 10–20% retained |
| Lab (Pick 10) | D-FL 1.5ct | IGI | 7.4mm | $4,916 | 10–20% retained |

The lab 2ct at $4,990 (Pick 7) gives you 25% more face-up size than any natural pick. The lab 1.5ct picks (8–10) land between the natural 1ct and the lab 2ct — 7.4mm face-up for $4,709–$4,916. All lab options trade resale value for size.

If this is a ring you will wear for 20 years and never sell, the lab wins on every metric except the cert. If this is a ring you want to pass on or liquidate at some point, natural GIA is the only answer.

The 1ct natural at $4,530 (Pick 1) is not a small stone. It is 6.5mm — proportionate, certified, correct. Most men who choose the lab 2ct at $4,990 do so for size. Most men who choose the natural 1ct at $4,530 do so for the cert.

Both are right answers. Know which one you are before you spend.

